# toolbox, suppressions

Every `# nosec`, `noqa`, `type: ignore` or equivalent in this repository, with
the question that was asked and the answer that was given. **A suppression that
is not here is a defect**, because the register is the only thing standing
between a silenced check and nobody remembering why.

This file did not exist until 2026-09-04, and
`docs/DECISIONS/no-suppressions-file-while-nothing-is-silenced.md` was correct
until the same day: nothing was silenced, so a register would have been a
document about an empty set. Two things were silenced within an hour of each
other and the decision expired exactly as it said it would.

## S-1, bandit's subprocess findings in the test tree

**Thirteen marks, two rules, six files, and every one is a test.** `B404` for
importing `subprocess`, `B603` for calling it without `shell=True`.

    tests/conftest.py                         # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/conftest.py                         # nosec B603    the flag-contract runner
    tests/test_bolt_result_adapter.py         # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_bolt_result_adapter.py         # nosec B603    running the adapter
    tests/test_coverage_adapter.py            # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_coverage_adapter.py       ×2   # nosec B603    the run helper, and the two-evidence case
    tests/test_gofmt_adapter.py               # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_gofmt_adapter.py               # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_suppression_register.py        # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_suppression_register.py        # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case
    tests/test_traceability.py                # nosec B404    import subprocess
    tests/test_traceability.py                # nosec B603    the script-runs-as-a-script case

**It was sixteen across seven files an hour earlier**, and the three that went
are the useful part of this entry. The Rust and Python coverage adapter tests
each carried their own eighteen-line runner, identical apart from the evidence
filename, and pylint's R0801 said so. Extracting it to `conftest.py`'s
`run_flag_adapter` removed a copy of the subprocess call along with the
duplication, and this checker then failed until the register followed — which is
what the register is for.

**The rows carry the pragma as it is spelled in the source, and `×N` where a
file holds more than one.** That is what makes this an index rather than a
description of one: the checker reads these rows with the same patterns it scans
the source with, so a row naming no recognised pragma is prose to it.

**Why the marks rather than an exclusion.** The Python jig runs bandit twice:
once over the tree without its tests, held to everything, and once over the test
tree alone with `--skip B101`. Its comment says why B404 and B603 are not also
skipped there — "deliberate subprocess use is a design choice worth seeing
rather than noise to silence". Marking each site is what that sentence asks for.
Excluding the test tree would have been one line and would have stopped anyone
ever seeing a new one appear.

**Why the subprocess calls exist at all**, which is the question the register is
for. A checker or adapter is a script bolt spawns, so its shebang, its imports
and where `main()` writes its envelope are part of the contract, and an
in-process call proves none of them. Every `B603` above is a test running the
real script the way bolt runs it. `tests/conftest.py`'s `script_argv` is where
they all go through, and it also routes them under `coverage run
--parallel-mode`, without which a spawned script reports 0% with a full suite
behind it.

Line numbers are deliberately not recorded. They go stale on the next edit and a
stale line number is worse than none, because it sends a reader to the wrong
call. `grep -rn nosec tests/ adapters/ bin/` is the current list and it is one
command.

## S-2, the standard XML parser in the Cobertura adapter

**Two marks, one file.**

    adapters/python/coverage.py    # nosec B405    import xml.etree.ElementTree
    adapters/python/coverage.py    # nosec B314    ET.parse on the report

`defusedxml` is not a dependency here, so the adapter uses the standard parser.
That is defensible in exactly one circumstance and this is it: the document it
parses was written by the `tests` task in the same jig, seconds earlier, in the
same work directory. It is not a document arriving from anywhere.

**What would retire this entry.** Either `defusedxml` becomes a declared
dependency — which `requires:` cannot express today, since it names executables
and not libraries, and which anvil's NEXT_STEPS carries as its first item — or
the adapter stops parsing XML. Neither is close, so the marks stay and this
paragraph is what a reader gets instead.

The module docstring carries the same reasoning at the point of use, which is
deliberate duplication: a reader of the adapter should not have to find this
file to know why the parser is the standard one.

## S-3, duplication between scripts that cannot import each other

**Nine marks, one rule, every standalone script here.**

    bin/test-traceability.py       # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    bin/suppression-register.py    # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    bin/link-toolbox.py            # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/common/bolt-result.py # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/go/coverage.py        # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/go/gofmt.py           # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/go/govet.py           # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/python/coverage.py    # pylint: disable=duplicate-code
    adapters/rust/coverage.py      # pylint: disable=duplicate-code

**This is structural rather than incidental, and it was met three times before
being written down as one thing.** Every script here is spawned by path, from a
directory that is not a package, and linked one file at a time into each
adopter. None can import another. So anything two of them must both do is
written twice, and R0801 reports a different pair each time one is dissolved:

    the coverage adapters' judgement    the two thresholds, the per-file rule,
                                        the statistics, the envelope, `main`
    the checkers' SKIP_DIRS             the directories neither reads
    the adapters' emit                  eight lines writing `output.yaml`

Dissolving them one at a time is the wrong shape of work: three edits, three
justifications, and a fourth pair surfacing behind them.

**One of the three is already guarded by a test, which is the standard the
others should reach.**
`tests/test_suppression_register.py::test_both_checkers_skip_the_same_directories`
compares the two skip lists and fails if they diverge. That answers R0801's real
concern — silent divergence — with something that can fail, rather than with a
comment.

**What would retire this entry.** A shared module under `adapters/common/` and
another under `bin/`, joined to the `common` set so every adopter links them,
imported by path from scripts that currently import nothing but the standard
library and PyYAML. That is a real change: it puts a module behind nine shipped
scripts, adds a link every adopter takes whether or not it runs the task that
needs it, and reaches the Go coverage adapter, which judges no branches and does
not fit the same shape without thought. `NEXT_STEPS.md` carries it.

**What must not happen is the third option**: raising `min-similarity-lines`
until the findings stop. That is
`docs/LESSONS/a-dead-threshold-is-a-latent-relaxation.md` exactly, and it would
also silence the case R0801 got right this week — two test files that had copied
a subprocess block, which was dissolved rather than marked.
